  "details": "1. Use 6-point tracking; mine is head, hands, hip, and feet, with \"Lock Head\" enabled.\n\n2. Set horizon adjust to any non-zero value. I primarily tested this with 90-degree snapping to 180 degrees but was able to reproduce the issue at many angles.\n\n3.  have \"FBT locomotion\" enabled and begin moving.\n\nWhen all of the above are true, starting to move while at an angle forces my avatar's feet to \"snap\" to the downward direction, yanking the rest of my body with it. This is extremely disconcerting as both the user and viewer and renders horizon adjust essentially useless for me unless I am standing completely still (unlikely). I use horizon adjust regularly for role-playing scenarios for situations like rappelling, spider-climb magic, or magnetic boots in my sci-fi setting, and must turn off locomotion to do so, which breaks the illusion entirely. This also requires frequently enabling or disabling locomotion animations, which is an annoying process to repeatedly do.",
